---
title: AI Agents · Reading the log
key: ai-agents
description: "Where an Orcraft run writes its log, which tables an AI agent reads from it, and what the AI agent can do with the log afterwards."
---
<h1>Reading the log</h1>
<p>A run writes its log to a <code>Logs</code> folder in the working directory, one
  <code>.ansi</code> file per component. The children of a group, suite or batch are filed in a
  folder named after the parent's log file. The console shows the same text.</p>

<h2 id="tables">Log tables</h2>
<p>The log is made of titled tables. The titles are what the AI agent searches for:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Table</th>
      <th>Content</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>&lt;name&gt; | Configuration</code></td>
      <td>Every config field with the value the run used, including the endpoints.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Case N | Result</code></td>
      <td>One case: its status, times, the description of any failure, error or warning, and the
        test's own <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/result-fields/">result fields</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>&lt;name&gt; | Result</code></td>
      <td>The component's summary: status, finish time, elapsed time and case counts.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>&lt;group&gt; | Tests</code>, <code>&lt;suite&gt; | Groups</code>,
        <code>&lt;batch&gt; | Items</code></td>
      <td>One row per child with its status, counts and the endpoints it ran on.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>&lt;name&gt; | Failures</code>, <code>&lt;name&gt; | Errors</code>,
        <code>&lt;name&gt; | Warnings</code></td>
      <td>The issues collected during the run, with the step and case they came from.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="statuses">Statuses</h2>
<p>A status is <code class="status-pass">PASS</code>, <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> or
  <code class="status-error">ERROR</code>. The
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/">Statuses</a> page explains the difference between
  the last two, which is what an analysis turns on. The log of a group, suite or batch also carries one
  <code>Started</code> and one <code>Finished</code> line per test, with status, elapsed time and
  endpoints in fixed columns.</p>

<h2 id="analysis">Analysis</h2>
<p>The log is the complete record of the run, so the AI agent that ran the test can also analyze it:
  explain a failure from its step and case, compare two runs of the same test on two machines, or
  summarize a batch. The result of a run started this way lives in this log only. It does not appear
  in the controller's history or in its details dialog.</p>

<h2 id="export-for-agents">Export for Agents</h2>
<p>Runs made from the controller can be handed to the AI agent the other way round. The details dialog
  of any scope, from one test run to the whole session, has an <strong>Export for Agents</strong> button
  beside the spreadsheet export. It writes one folder under the controller's <code>Agents</code> folder,
  or in the folder chosen in its dialog, with:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>summary.txt</code>: the scope's configuration and results as plain text, written
    failure-first. A failed or errored row keeps its whole subtree of flows, steps and descriptions; a
    passed row stays one line with its parameters and result fields, and the subtrees of passed cases
    and passed setup and teardown stages are dropped. It ends with the list of runs in the scope and
    where each log is in the folder.</li>
  <li><code>logs/</code>: the log of every run the dialog shows, laid out as under the controller's
    <code>Logs</code> folder, with the colour codes stripped so each file is plain text.</li>
</ul>
<p>The AI agent reads the folder like the log of a run it started itself: the same tables, the same
  statuses. Nothing in the folder is a prompt or an instruction; what to ask about it is the request.</p>
